WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.490
SPEAKER 1: Today, we're talking
about so serverless exports,

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:06.820
Windows Server VMs, and
regular expressions.

00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:10.360
I'm Priyanka Vergadia, and
welcome to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.490
where we serve you
the lowest latency

00:00:12.490 --> 00:00:15.050
news from the safety of home.

00:00:15.050 --> 00:00:17.470
Whether you're maintaining
backups or feeding

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:21.130
downstream analytics, exporting
data out of your databases

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:23.800
can put a strain on
your production systems.

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.560
In order to make it easier
to schedule and maintain

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:29.320
these data exports, we've
launched a new feature

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.330
for Cloud SQL,
serverless exports.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.700
Severless exports enables you
to export data from your MySQL

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.920
and PostgreSQL
database instances

00:00:38.920 --> 00:00:41.800
without any impact, or
performance, or risk

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:44.020
to your production workloads.

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:46.240
Google Cloud is
dedicated to providing

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.720
a first-class experience for
migrating and modernizing

00:00:49.720 --> 00:00:51.160
Windows workloads.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.430
That's why we have just
announced several new features,

00:00:54.430 --> 00:00:57.760
all designed to make it easier
to manage your Windows Server

00:00:57.760 --> 00:00:58.990
workloads.

00:00:58.990 --> 00:01:01.570
There are three new
features in beta--

00:01:01.570 --> 00:01:06.100
boot screen diagnostics, auto
upgrades for Windows 2008,

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:08.590
and diagnostic
information collection.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:12.730
In addition, we've released a
tool designed around improved

00:01:12.730 --> 00:01:14.950
licensing reporting.

00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:17.920
The most daunting part
of cloud computing

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:21.040
is often the idea of
migrating to the cloud.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:23.650
That's why these solution
architects collected

00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:27.160
their advice and best practices
into a comprehensive set

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:30.820
of documents for the teams
who are planning, designing,

00:01:30.820 --> 00:01:33.850
and implementing a
migration to Google Cloud.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.580
This blog post provides
a high-level outline

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:40.240
of their guidance with links
to the relevant documentation.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.180
Finally, Cloud Logging
just got an upgrade

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:46.930
to support regular expressions
in our query language.

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:49.660
To celebrate this moment,
the software engineering

00:01:49.660 --> 00:01:53.230
lead for this project sat
down to share his best

00:01:53.230 --> 00:01:56.020
tips for getting the
most from your logs.

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.690
Check out the interview
in the blog post.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:00.910
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:02:00.910 --> 00:02:03.850
to see a full list, check
out the links below.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:05.390
Any thoughts or ideas?

00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:07.340
Leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:09.900
We'll see you in the cloud.

